  "notes": [
   "Parts are unchanged from step 1.",
   "Only the single-balloon circuit shown in the images is built; the optional second balloon (GPIO 3) is not drawn and is not included.",
   "BC635 was not found by lookup_part, so a new type 'transistor-bc635' (ports E, C, B) is defined. Flat side facing the ground rail: E in column 10, C in column 11, B in column 12 of row e.",
   "Breadboard naming: the image's numbered rows 1-30 are the catalogue's columns, and the letters a-e / f-j are its rows. The left rail pair is modelled as tp (red, +9V) and tn (black, ground). Exact rail index numbers (tp.7, tn.1, tn.2, tn.11, tp.30, tn.30) are approximate, since each rail is one strip.",
   "Pi header pins: GPIO 2 = pin 3, GND = pin 6, GPIO 14 = pin 8. The image shows a 40-pin header board, modelled as raspberry-pi-4-gpio.",
   "The pushbutton straddles the centre gap: side 1 in column 1 (1t.e / 1b.f) goes to ground, and side 2 in column 3 (3t.e / 3b.f) goes to GPIO 14. The input is read with the Pi's internal pull-up (gpiozero Button), so no external resistor is used.",
   "The 12\u03a9 balloon resistor r1 is off the breadboard, on the balloon. It is joined by twisting its legs to the two cores of the speaker cable (wire7, wire8). wire7 plugs into the collector row (column 11) and wire8 into column 6, which is linked by the red wire6 to the 9V rail.",
   "The 330\u03a9 resistor r2 runs from the transistor base row (column 12) to column 16, where the green wire from GPIO 2 plugs in.",
   "The 9V battery connects to the snap clip. The snap's red lead goes to the + rail and its black lead to the \u2212 rail."
  ]
